trace.content:
[THOUGHT] Physical exam confirms active gingival oozing and multifocal ecchymoses — consistent with a systemic hemostatic defect. Absence of petechiae makes severe thrombocytopenia less likely; absence of active joint swelling doesn’t rule out historical hemarthroses (per family history). Next, **Domain 8: Most informative laboratory / imaging** is now critical. Given the clinical picture — post-procedural hemorrhage + easy bruising + positive family history of bleeding *and* joint swelling — the highest-yield initial tests are coagulation studies:  
- **PT (prothrombin time)** and **aPTT (activated partial thromboplastin time)** to localize the defect (aPTT prolongation suggests intrinsic pathway — e.g., factor VIII/IX/XI deficiency);  
- **Platelet count**, to exclude thrombocytopenia;  
- **vWF antigen & activity** and **factor VIII level**, given high suspicion for von Willebrand disease or hemophilia A — but since vWF testing is more specialized and may not be first-tier in acute setting, I’ll start with screening tests that are universally available and highly discriminatory.  
PT and aPTT are foundational and will guide next steps.

[TEST] PT (Prothrombin Time) and aPTT (activated Partial Thromboplastin Time)

